WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 109: Queen Liliuokalani: The Overthrow of the Hawaiian Kingdom

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:20.240
[Rebecca] Today, we step into the grand, elegant halls of Ayolani Palace in Honolulu, Hawaii, to witness one of the most heartbreaking, controversial, and shameful acts of imperial aggression in 19th century history.

4
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:27.200
[Rebecca] The illegal overthrow of Queen Lilia Wakalani and the Kingdom of Hawaii in January 1893.

5
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:42.720
[Rebecca] Highly educated, musically gifted, and deeply beloved by her people, Queen Lilia Wakalani ascended the throne determined to overturn the humiliating, coerced bayonet constitution and restore democratic voting rights to Native Hawaiians.

6
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:52.240
[Rebecca] But standing against her was a ruthless cabal of American sugar oligarchs, backed by the unauthorized military power of United States Marines.

7
00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:00.720
[Rebecca] Rather than spill the blood of her people, the Queen yielded her authority under protest to the United States government, trusting in American justice.

8
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:09.440
[Rebecca] What followed was a tragic struggle that culminated in her house arrest in her own palace, composing the immortal farewell song, Aloha O.

9
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:15.200
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full, poignant chronicle.

10
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:32.400
[George] Born Lydia Liliu Laloku Walenia Wewehi Kamakaha on September 2nd, 1838, into one of Hawaii's highest Ali noble families, Queen Lilia Wakalani was an extraordinary woman of intellect, artistic brilliance, and profound spiritual depth.

11
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:47.360
[George] Raised in accordance with traditional Hawaiian Hanai custom, she was formally educated alongside future monarchs at the royal school by American Protestant missionaries, mastering English, Latin, Mathematics, and European royal protocol.

12
00:01:47.360 --> 00:02:01.560
[George] Lilia Wakalani was a gifted musical virtuoso who played the organ, piano, and guitar, composing over 150 sacred hymns and traditional meal chants that remain treasured cornerstones of Hawaiian cultural heritage.

13
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:17.720
[George] Her most celebrated composition, written in 1878 following a horseback ride on Oahu, was Aloha O Farewell to Thee, a hauntingly beautiful melody whose poignant lyrics of parting lovers would later become the tragic anthem of a stolen nation.

14
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:31.240
[George] In 1891, upon the death of her beloved brother, King David Kalicoa, Lileauakalani took the royal oath inside Ayolani Palace, ascending the throne as the eighth and final monarch of the Hawaiian kingdom.

15
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:43.880
[George] She inherited a proud, internationally recognized sovereign nation that had treaties of friendship with over twenty foreign powers, including the United States, Great Britain, France, and Imperial Germany.

16
00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:50.840
[George] Yet beneath the glittering surface of the royal court, a deadly political cancer was consuming the kingdom's sovereignty.

17
00:02:50.840 --> 00:03:02.280
[George] Liliawakalani's deep Christian faith and mastery of Western literature, diplomacy and classical music made her one of the most intellectually accomplished monarchs of the Victorian era.

18
00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:14.840
[George] The primary threat to Hawaiian sovereignty came from a small, wealthy minority of white American and European missionary descendants, merchants and sugar barons who dominated the island's agricultural economy.

19
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:26.200
[George] Under lucrative trade reciprocity treaties with the United States, immense sugar plantations boomed across the islands, generating colossal fortunes for American oligarchs like Sanford B.

20
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.680
[George] Dole, Lauren Thurston, and the Big Five trading conglomerates.

21
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.680
[George] However, the planters were not content with economic dominance.

22
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.400
[George] They demanded total political control.

23
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:48.000
[George] In July 1887, a secretive paramilitary organization of white merchants known as the Honolulu Rifles staged an armed coup d'etat against King Kalakoa.

24
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.560
[George] Cornering the king in his palace at gunpoint, they forced him to sign the humiliating Bayonet Constitution.

25
00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:02.960
[George] The Bayonet Constitution stripped the Hawaiian monarchy of virtually all executive power, transforming the king into a powerless figurehead.

26
00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:14.560
[George] Worse still, it established exorbitant property and income requirements for voting, disenfranchising over 75% of Native Hawaiian citizens and all Asian residents.

27
00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:21.200
[George] While granting the vote to foreign American and European residents who refused to take an oath of allegiance to Hawaii.

28
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.680
[George] Native Hawaii watched in anguish as control of their own government was stolen by a wealthy foreign minority.

29
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:37.760
[George] The Bayonet Constitution represented a blatant, armed coup by foreign commercial interests determined to subjugate native sovereignty for private agricultural profits.

30
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:57.600
[George] When Queen Liliawakalani ascended the throne in 1891, native Hawaiian citizens flooded Ayolani Palace with over 6,000 petitions signed in every district across the islands, begging the Queen to promulgate a new constitution that would abolish the Bayonet Constitution and restore their democratic franchise.

31
00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:07.680
[George] Liliawakalani recognized that continuing the minority rule of foreign sugar planters would inevitably lead to the total economic and cultural subjugation of her people.

32
00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:18.400
[George] She drafted a proposed constitution that restored royal executive authority, reduced voting property qualifications, and strictly limited the vote to Hawaiian citizens.

33
00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.000
[George] It was a constitution that was supported by an overwhelming 90% of the kingdom's populace.

34
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:37.000
[George] On Saturday afternoon, January 14, 1893, following the formal prorogation of the Legislative Assembly, the Queen prepared to sign the new constitution inside Ayalani Palace.

35
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:46.360
[George] However, her cabinet ministers, intimidated by threats of violence from white merchants, hesitated and pleaded with the Queen to delay the proclamation.

36
00:05:46.360 --> 00:06:02.520
[George] Recognizing that proceeding without unified cabinet support could provoke civil strife, Lileauakalani reluctantly postponed the signing, addressing thousands of disappointed Hawaiian subjects gathered on the palace lawn, promising that their rights would soon be restored.

37
00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:12.840
[George] Over 90% of native Hawaiian citizens signed emotional petitions begging their queen to restore the sacred democratic rights stolen by the missionary sugar oligarchy.

38
00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:20.520
[George] The Queen's attempt to restore democratic governance provided the pretext that the American sugar oligarchs had long been waiting for.

39
00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:29.080
[George] A cabal of thirteen white businessmen, led by attorney Lauren Thurston, immediately formed the treasonous Committee of Safety.

40
00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:43.560
[George] Their goal was not merely to stop the new constitution, but to overthrow the sovereign Hawaiian monarchy and annex the islands to the United States, allowing Hawaiian sugar to enter the American market free of McKinley tariff penalties.

41
00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.880
[George] Crucial to the conspiracy was John L.

42
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.320
[George] Stevens, the United States minister to Hawaii.

43
00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:59.720
[George] Stevens was a fervent, expansionist imperialist who had previously written to the State Department describing Hawaii as a ripe pair, ready to be plucked by the American Eagle.

44
00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:05.160
[George] Working in direct conspiracy with the Committee of Safety, Stevens took a treasonous step.

45
00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:22.080
[George] On Monday afternoon, January 16, 1893, under the false pretense of protecting American lives and property, he ordered United States Navy Captain Gilbert Wiltsey to land armed military forces from the cruiser USS Boston anchored in Honolulu Harbor.

46
00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:32.400
[George] 162 heavily armed United States Marines and sailors, equipped with gattling guns and field artillery, marched through the streets of Honolulu.

47
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:48.560
[George] Instead of guarding the American legation, the troops took up offensive military positions directly across the street from Ayalani Palace and the Royal Government Building, training their weapons directly at the Queen's residence in an unmistakable display of military intimidation.

48
00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:59.920
[George] The unauthorized landing of United States Marines with gattling guns and artillery directly outside the Royal Palace constituted a flagrant act of international military intimidation.

49
00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:16.080
[George] On the afternoon of Tuesday, January 17, 1893, the Committee of Safety entered the government building under the protective shadow of American cannons and proclaimed the establishment of a provisional government of Hawaii, appointing jurist Sanford B.

50
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.080
[George] Dole as president.

51
00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:28.400
[George] Minutes later, Minister Stevens committed a flagrant violation of international law by formally recognizing the provisional government as the de facto authority of Hawaii.

52
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:41.280
[George] Before the Queen had surrendered and while the Royal Palace remained garrisoned by 600 loyal Royal Guards, the Queen's military commanders and native Hawaiian leaders begg Liliawakalani to allow them to fight.

53
00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:48.240
[George] They argued that their 600 armed guards and thousands of loyal warriors could easily overwhelm the white conspirators.

54
00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.200
[George] But Lileawakalani was a devout Christian and a devoted mother to her people.

55
00:08:53.200 --> 00:09:04.440
[George] She knew that any armed resistance would lead to a bloody massacre by United States Marine artillery, resulting in the slaughter of hundreds of young Hawaiian men and the bombardment of Honolulu.

56
00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:18.040
[George] Choosing moral strength over bloodshed, Queen Lileauakalani issued her historic, formal declaration yielding her authority not to the illegal provisional government, but under protest to the United States of America.

57
00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:26.280
[George] Now to avoid any collision of armed forces, and perhaps the loss of life, I do under this protest, and impelled by said force.

58
00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:33.480
[George] Yield my authority until such time as the government of the United States shall, upon facts being presented to it.

59
00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:41.320
[George] Undo the action of its representative and reinstate me in the authority which I claim as the constitutional sovereign of the Hawaiian Islands.

60
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:52.840
[George] Rather than sacrifice the lives of her young royal guards against American naval artillery, Queen Liliawakalani surrendered her authority under protest, trusting in American justice.

61
00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:58.840
[George] Queen Liliawakalani's faith in American constitutional justice was initially vindicated.

62
00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:11.880
[George] When President Grover Cleveland took office in Washington in March 1893, he immediately withdrew the annexation treaty submitted by the conspirators, denouncing the overthrow as an act of international piracy.

63
00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.160
[George] Cleveland dispatched former Congressman James H.

64
00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:20.200
[George] Blount to Honolulu to conduct an exhaustive, independent investigation.

65
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:24.920
[George] In July 1893, the historic Blount Report was released.

66
00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.240
[George] Blount's findings were blistering.

67
00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:43.320
[George] He concluded that United States Minister Stevens had conspired with a small minority of foreign planters to execute an illegal armed coup against a friendly, sovereign nation, and that an overwhelming majority of native Hawaiians opposed annexation and supported their Queen.

68
00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:58.960
[George] President Cleveland addressed a joint session of Congress, declaring that the overthrow was an act of war, committed with the participation of a diplomatic representative of the United States and without authority of Congress, demanding that the lawful monarch be restored.

69
00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:08.800
[George] However, Sanford Dole and the sugar oligarchs flatly refused to step down, declaring the Republic of Hawaii on July 4, 1894.

70
00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:18.400
[George] In January 1895, Loyalist Hawaiian patriots staged an armed counter-revolution to restore their queen, but the uprising was crushed.

71
00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:36.000
[George] Queen Lilia Wakalani was arrested, subjected to a humiliating military show trial inside her own throne room, and sentenced to five years of hard labor and a $5,000 fine, later commuted to eight months of strict imprisonment in an upstairs bedroom of Ayolani Palace.

72
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:46.160
[George] The exhaustive findings of the Blount Report conclusively proved that the Queen had been unlawfully deposed through the treasonous conspiracy of Minister Stevens and foreign planters.

73
00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:56.160
[George] During her eight months of imprisonment in Ayalani Palace, denied newspapers and visitors, Queen Lileauakalani displayed breathtaking grace and fortitude.

74
00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:04.080
[George] She composed music, hand stitched an intricate commemorative silk quilt recording the history of her reign, and prayed for her people.

75
00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:13.920
[George] In 1897, following the election of expansionist President William McKinley, a renewed annexation treaty was sent to the United States Senate.

76
00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:23.680
[George] In response, native Hawaiian patriotic organizations mounted one of the most heroic civic resistance campaigns in world history, the Cup Petitions.

77
00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:39.800
[George] Traveling by horseback, canoe and foot across every island, Hawaiian organizers collected the signatures of 21,269 Native Hawaiian citizens, over 90% of the adult native population, formally opposing annexation.

78
00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:50.760
[George] Queen Lilia Wakalani traveled personally to Washington, D.C., presenting the colossal petitions to the United States Senate, successfully killing the annexation treaty.

79
00:12:50.760 --> 00:13:06.520
[George] Yet in 1898, during the imperial hysteria of the Spanish-American War, McKinley bypassed the constitutional two-thirds treaty requirement, annexing Hawaii via the controversial Newlands Resolution, a simple joint resolution of Congress.

80
00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:17.880
[George] Queen Lilia Wakalani lived out her remaining years at Washington Place in Honolulu, fighting tirelessly for her people's welfare until her death on November 11, 1917.

81
00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:27.800
[George] In 1993, 100 years after the coup, the United States Congress passed and President Bill Clinton signed the historic apology resolution.

82
00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:35.080
[George] Formally apologizing to Native Hawaiians for the illegal overthrow of their kingdom and the suppression of their inherent sovereignty.

83
00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:42.600
[George] Today, Queen Lileauakalani stands as an immortal symbol of dignity, forgiveness, and cultural resilience.

84
00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:51.160
[George] Her spirit lives on in every verse of Aloha O, reminding the world of a sovereign Hawaiian nation that never truly surrendered its soul.

85
00:13:51.160 --> 00:14:03.160
[George] The Ka Petitions, containing over 21,000 verified native signatures, stand in the National Archives as a monumental testament to Hawaii's unyielding patriotic resistance.

86
00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:08.120
[Rebecca] Queen Lilia Wakalani's grace, moral dignity, and immortal song.

87
00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:13.880
[Rebecca] Aloha'o continue to inspire the Hawaiian people and lovers of freedom across the globe.

88
00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:16.720
[Rebecca] That concludes our epic 50-state journey.

89
00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:17.920
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

90
00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:25.760
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we inaugurate our new series: The Fascinating, Untold Stories of the Presidents of the United States.

